Goblins-shaped `session` surface with native values
([`pancake.rkt`](https://codeberg.org/jimisaacs/pancake/src/branch/main/bindings/racket/pancake.rkt)).
Transports: `pancake-tcp.rkt`, `pancake-inproc.rkt`, lazy `pancake-iroh.rkt`.
Install: [Bindings]($link.page('bindings')). Actor vocabulary:
[modules]($link.page('modules')).

## The same round-trip

The [Start building]($link.page('start')) round-trip (connect, fetch, send,
handle the reply) reads like this in Racket. The local is `sess` so it does not
shadow `session-fetch` / `session-send`.

> []($block.attrs('code-tabs'))
> **tcp**
> ```scheme
> (require "pancake.rkt")
>
> ;; tcp: the peer is a place, a cleartext host and port.
> (define partner (tcp-locator "127.0.0.1" 9099))
>
> (define sess (connect partner))
> (define greeter (session-fetch sess "greeter")) ; a capability, by name
> (printf "~a\n" (session-send sess greeter 'greet "world"))
> (session-close! sess)
> ```
> **iroh**
> ```scheme
> (require "pancake.rkt"
>          "pancake-iroh.rkt")
>
> ;; iroh reachability: the peer is a key, not a place. IROH-REACH-DISCOVERY
> ;; resolves the bare node id over the open internet; no address in hand.
> (define partner "ocapn://3b6a…da29.iroh")
>
> (define sess (connect partner IROH-REACH-DISCOVERY))
> (define greeter (session-fetch sess "greeter")) ; a capability, by name
> (printf "~a\n" (session-send sess greeter 'greet "world"))
> (session-close! sess)
> ```

The iroh tab is a bare node id plus posture; fetch/send match the tcp tab.
Dial-by-id and discovery: [iroh]($link.page('transports/iroh')).

## A Goblins-faithful actor layer

Racket keeps the Goblins verbs intact. You define an actor constructor with
`(define (^name bcom arg ...) ...)`, return its `methods` behavior, and then
`spawn` it. You call it near with `$`, send it eventually with `<-` or `<-np`,
observe a promise with `on`, and become a new behavior with `bcom`, all inside
`with-vat`. On top of that, `connect`, `session-fetch`, and `session-send` reach
a partner, and `(accept (listen bind_host port))` hands back the same `session`
that `connect` returns.

> **Near-call (`$`) and eventual-send (`<-`)**: the two ways to invoke an object,
> straight from the [E language](http://erights.org/). `$` calls a near object now
> and returns its result, because it lives in your own vat. `<-` queues the call
> for a later turn and hands back a promise (`<-np` when you do not need the
> promise back). It is the only way to reach across a session, since a partner's
> object is never in your vat.

## Async

Green threads and ports. `(session-serve sess)` pumps inbound; `(on vow …)`
fires on settle. CapTP ideas: [Concepts]($link.page('concepts')).

## Binding check and example

```sh
zig build racket-dogfood    # the Racket consumer against the pancake archives
```

The full surface lives in the
[Racket binding README](https://codeberg.org/jimisaacs/pancake/src/branch/main/bindings/racket/README.md),
and the
[rooms chat client](https://codeberg.org/jimisaacs/pancake/src/branch/main/examples/apps/rooms/racket/room-client.rkt)
builds a small capability-secure room on this surface.
